<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Enrique Araujo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Enrique Araujo was a Salvadoran physician and president from 1911 to 1913. His government founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>, a rural police force, and was remembered for its relative tolerance and reforms. He was attacked with machetes in a park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> in February 1913 and died of his wounds days later, opening the way for the Meléndez family's rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-enrique-araujo.jpg" alt="Manuel Enrique Araujo" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Enrique_Araujo_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel Enrique Araujo Rodríguez was born on 12 October 1865 at Hacienda Condadillo, Estanzuelas, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Usulután, the youngest of eight children of a Basque father and a Portuguese mother. He studied at the University of El Salvador and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and practised as a surgeon in the 1890s and 1900s. In 1887 he married María Hortensia Peralta Lagos, daughter of the former acting president José María Peralta. In 1907 he was elected vice president under Fernando Figueroa with 95.47 percent of the vote, and both took office on 1 March 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Chosen by Figueroa as his successor, Araujo won the 1911 election with 182,964 votes; the historian Alastair White remarked that opponents were allowed to take part but not to win. He took office on 1 March 1911, the first civilian president since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-zaldivar">Rafael Zaldívar</a> was deposed in 1885. Fearing a coup backed by the Guatemalan president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Manuel Estrada Cabrera</a>, he cultivated popular support, visiting schools, prisons and hospitals. His Work Accidents Law made the state and employers share compensation for disabled workers, though not rural labourers; he also allowed the direct taxation of property and abolished imprisonment for debt. He began the National Theater in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>.</p><p>In June 1912 he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>, modelled on the Spanish Civil Guard, and rural patrols of retired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>. His government used both to repress peasants who criticised it and to defend allied landowners; the Guard remained an instrument of repression until it was abolished in 1992. Araujo denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>' military intervention in Nicaragua as a great scandal and, when President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a> protested, replied that he obeyed no one's orders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 4 February 1913, sitting without an escort in Bolívar Park, he was attacked by three men with machetes and a revolver. He died on 9 February after his wounds became infected. Three farmers were shot on 19 February; the man they named, Major Fernando Carmona, killed himself in prison. The former vice president Prudencio Alfaro was executed in 1915 for an alleged role, and White judged that Araujo was probably killed on Alfaro's orders. Some 15,000 people attended his funeral. He remains the only Salvadoran president assassinated in office.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Enrique Araujo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alegría, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.51,-88.49&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Alegría on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2125 1661 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manuel Enrique Araujo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2575 1931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2575 1931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.51,-88.49&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
